                <pb facs="0128" n="98" xml:id="pg98"/>

                <head>48. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>In early September 1953 CIA officers met with the man known to be Castillo Armas’
                    main ally inside of Guatemala City. The information received by the CIA in New
                    York on September 6–7 differed from the assessment of internal support described
                    in <ref target="#d47">Document 47</ref>. According to the source, there already
                    were 53,000 anti-Communist Guatemalans organized in cells of 315 men inside the
                    country, approximately 8,000 supporters within 1 hour of the capital, and two
                    functional intelligence nets. Other assets listed by the source included five
                    clandestine radio stations and ample supplies for the manufacture of bombs. He
                    noted, however: “Capture of Guatemala City at the outset of the movement will
                    require the immobilization or assassination of the key civilian members of the
                    Government as well as immobilization of a majority of the officers.”</p>
                <p>At the conclusion of the meeting, the source agreed that his organization would
                    prepare a detailed plan and timetable while the CIA would provide 8–10 tons of
                    arms for training purposes. (Memorandum for the Record, September 8; Central
                    Intelligence Agency, Job 79–01025A, Box 151, Folder 7)</p>
            </div>
